## Diagnosis
The failing test `testArrayEquals` in `MathUtilsTest` checks the behavior of the `equals` method in `MathUtils` when comparing arrays containing `Double.NaN` values. The test expects `MathUtils.equals` to return `false` when comparing two arrays containing a single `Double.NaN` value each. However, the current bug report highlights that the behavior of `MathUtils.equals` method contradicts the IEEE standard by returning `true` in this scenario.

By inspecting the implementation of the `MathUtils.equals` method, we can determine whether the method incorrectly returns `true` when comparing arrays containing `Double.NaN`.

## Code Snippet - MathUtils.equals
```java
public static boolean equals(double[] left, double[] right) {
    if (left == right) {
        return true;
    }
    if (left == null || right == null) {
        return false;
    }
    if (left.length != right.length) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < left.length; i++) {
        if (Double.doubleToLongBits(left[i]) != Double.doubleToLongBits(right[i])) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

The `MathUtils.equals` method compares two arrays `left` and `right` by iterating over their elements and comparing them using `Double.doubleToLongBits`. This comparison may treat `Double.NaN` values as equal due to the IEEE 754 floating-point standard, leading to the incorrect behavior reported in the bug.

The issue can be localized to the comparison logic in the `equals` method, specifically in how `Double.NaN` values are handled during the comparison process.

